-- title:
--   Simple radical extensions with n invertible are finite separable of degree ≤ n
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ and $E$ be fields with $E$ an $F$-algebra, let $n$ be a natural number whose image in $F$ is nonzero, let $a \in E$, and let $b \in F$ be nonzero with $a^n$ equal to the image of $b$ under the structure map $F \to E$. Assume further that the intermediate field of $E/F$ generated by the single element $a$ is all of $E$ (i.e. `IntermediateField.adjoin F {a} = ⊤`). The conclusion is the conjunction of three assertions: $E$ is a finite-dimensional $F$-module; the $F$-rank of $E$, as given by `Module.finrank`, is at most $n$; and the extension $E/F$ is separable in the sense of `Algebra.IsSeparable`, i.e. every element of $E$ is separable over $F$. No root of unity is assumed to lie in $F$, and no irreducibility of $X^n - b$ is assumed, so the bound on the degree is an inequality rather than an equality.
--
--   This is the elementary degree and separability input for simple radical (Kummer-type) extensions: when $n$ is invertible in $F$, adjoining an $n$-th root of a nonzero element of $F$ gives a finite separable extension of degree at most $n$. It is used in the study of valuation-theoretic behaviour of such extensions, being cited by [`ValuationSubring.forall_comap_eq_imp_eq_and_exists_forall_sub_mem_nonunits_of_pow_eq_of_isCoprime`](thm.html#ValuationSubring.forall_comap_eq_imp_eq_and_exists_forall_sub_mem_nonunits_of_pow_eq_of_isCoprime).
